<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Masa Umasan — Halachah 1<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400;">COMPLETE FULL TRANSLATION</span></div>

<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="lr">LM I:69</span><br>The root of <i>nefashos</i> is in wealth; <i>masa umasan b'emunah</i>; the prohibition of partnership with idolaters</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Halachic framework</span>: "Afterward he shall go to his business, etc. It is forbidden to enter into a partnership with idolaters, lest a [situation requiring an] oath arise, and he would transgress <span class="v">'the name of other gods you shall not mention; it shall not be heard upon [your mouth],'</span> etc." (<span class="h">Shulchan Aruch, Orach Chaim</span> 155).</div>

<div class="sn">Introduction</div>

<div class="p">The matter is as follows: It is explained in the words of Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> (in <span class="lr">Siman 69, Part I</span>) that <span class="kc">the root of the <span class="h">nefashos</span> [souls] is in wealth</span>. The essential magnification of the <span class="h">shem</span> [name] and the <span class="h">nefesh</span> [soul] is through holy wealth (<span class="h">ashirus d'kedushah</span>), for holy wealth is exceedingly lofty — see there.</div>

<div class="p">It emerges that holy money — which is Jewish money — is the aspect of <span class="h">shem</span> [name] and <span class="h">nefesh</span> [soul]. And the <span class="h">shem</span> and <span class="h">nefesh</span> of Israel is the aspect of the Name of Hashem, for His Name is joined with our name. This is the aspect of <span class="v">"Did you conduct your business dealings with <span class="h">emunah</span> [faithfulness]?"</span> — "<span class="h">b'emunah</span>" specifically! For <span class="h">emunah</span> is the aspect of the Name of Hashem — the aspect of <span class="v">"Hashem is One, and His Name is One"</span> — which is the essence of <span class="h">emunah</span>. Therefore one must conduct business with <span class="h">emunah</span>, for the root of <span class="h">masa umasan</span> [business] is embedded within <span class="h">emunah</span>, which is the aspect of the Name of Hashem, since <span class="h">masa umasan</span> and wealth are the aspect of <span class="h">shem</span> as explained above.</div>

<div class="p">And <span class="v">"G-d made this corresponding to that."</span> For money of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> is the opposite: it is the aspect of the name of other gods, for all forms of idolatry are embedded in money, as Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> states elsewhere (in <span class="lr">Siman 23, Part I</span>). The totality of Jewish money is the aspect of holy money, which is <span class="h">shem</span> and <span class="h">nefesh</span>, the aspect of the Name of Hashem as above. And the totality of idolaters' money is money of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>, which is the aspect of actual <span class="h">avodah zarah</span> — the aspect of the name of other gods as above.</div>

<div class="p">Now it is well explained why our Sages <span class="h">zichronam livrachah</span> forbade entering into partnership with idolaters on account of <span class="v">"the name of other gods you shall not mention,"</span> etc. For certainly one violates <span class="v">"the name of other gods,"</span> etc., when one enters into partnership with idolaters, because the root of the <span class="h">nefashos</span> is in wealth. When one enters into partnership with idolaters, one links one's own <span class="h">nefesh</span> and the <span class="h">nefesh</span> of the idolater together, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, so that both are rooted together in one pool of money — the very place where the root of the <span class="h">nefashos</span> resides, as above. Therefore one certainly violates <span class="v">"the name of other gods,"</span> etc., for the <span class="h">nefesh</span> and money of the idolater — which is the root of his <span class="h">nefesh</span> — is the name of other gods. And <span class="v">"go, say to him"</span> [= depart from it!] — it has no right to be joined with Jewish money, which is the aspect of the <span class="h">shem</span> and <span class="h">nefesh</span> of Israel, in which the Name of Hashem is embedded.</div>

<div class="p">This is what our Sages <span class="h">zichronam livrachah</span> said: "lest a [situation requiring an] oath arise." For a holy oath is the aspect of the Name of Hashem, as it is written: <span class="v">"You shall not take the Name of Hashem [in vain],"</span> etc., and <span class="v">"you shall not swear by My Name [falsely],"</span> etc. Therefore, wherever there is a dispute between two people and it is unknown to whom the money belongs, an oath is imposed between them. The oath clarifies the truth — to whom the money belongs. For the oath is the aspect of the Name of Hashem, which is the root of money (which is the aspect of <span class="h">shem</span>, as above). Therefore through the oath, it becomes thoroughly clarified to whom the money belongs, since at its root, the money of each person is certainly well-clarified — no one can touch his fellow's [money] there at all.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore our Sages <span class="h">zichronam livrachah</span> were concerned lest the idolater be required to swear an oath. For this is something likely — close to certain. For idolaters are full of falsehood in business, because their money — which is money of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> — is taken from the aspect of <span class="h">sheker</span> [falsehood], which is <span class="h">avodah zarah</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">sheker</span>. Therefore they are always suspected of falsehood, as it is written: <span class="v">"whose mouth speaks vanity and whose right hand is a right hand of falsehood."</span> Therefore certainly the idolater will be required to swear their oath — which is the aspect of other gods, for from there is the root of their money, from the aspect of <span class="h">avodah zarah</span>, from the aspect of the name of other gods, which is the aspect of their false oath. Therefore it is forbidden for a Jew to enter into partnership with him, for Jewish money is holy money — the root of his <span class="h">shem</span> and <span class="h">nefesh</span>, in which the Name of Hashem is embedded, as above.</div>

<div class="p">See the discourse beginning <span class="v">"Whoever wishes to draw people close to Hashem,"</span> etc., where it is explained regarding the verse <span class="v">"I will give them in My house and within My walls a hand and a name [<span class="h">yad va'shem</span>]"</span> — there it explains well this principle that money is the aspect of <span class="h">shem</span> and <span class="h">nefesh</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="sep">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

<!-- ═══════════════ MASA UMASAN 2 (brief, bridging) ═══════════════ -->
<div class="halacha-title" style="margin-top:2rem;">Hilchos Masa Umasan — Halachah 2<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400;">COMPLETE FULL TRANSLATION</span></div>

<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="lr">LM I:35</span><br>Business as rest for the mind; prohibition of mentioning Hashem's Name in vain</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Halachic framework</span>: "After leaving the synagogue he shall go to the <span class="h">bais midrash</span>, etc., to study, etc." (Ibid. Siman 155). This has already been explained above: one must study after prayer in order to acquire life, etc. "And afterward he shall go to his business, for all Torah without work is destined to be nullified and brings sin. And he should be careful not to mention the Name of Heaven in vain, etc."</div>

<div class="p">For one must conduct business after study, in order to give rest (<span class="h">nichas</span>) to the mind so that it will not be overtaxed. As Rabbainu explains explicitly (in <span class="lr">Siman 35</span>): <span class="kc">business is the aspect of sleep, which gives rest to the mind.</span> For one must specifically give rest to the mind when it is fatigued, so that there will not be a situation of "excessive light causing the shattering of the vessels." For when one thinks excessively with one's intellect without giving it rest, the brain — which is the vessel of the intellect — cannot receive the abundant light of the intellect. Then the brain becomes completely ruined, and one can lose one's mind entirely.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="v">"all Torah without [work] is destined to be nullified [<span class="h">b'tailah</span>]."</span> For one comes to the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> [nullification/cessation], because the vessels cannot receive the excessive occupation with Torah. The excessive light causes the shattering of the vessels. Then the intellect exits from the <span class="h">tzimtzum</span> [contraction/boundary] and becomes completely nullified. Then the brain is ruined and collapses entirely — for the essential endurance of the mind depends on the vessels being able to receive the intellect and contract it within themselves.</div>

<div class="p">This is [the meaning of] <span class="v">"destined to be nullified and brings [<span class="h">goreres</span>] sin."</span> For when the intellect departs into <span class="h">bitul</span> due to the shattering of the vessels caused by excessive light, from this the <span class="h">kelippos</span> are formed — as is brought [in the writings of the Arizal]. This is <span class="v">"and brings sin."</span> And this is observable in reality: when the brain is overtaxed excessively and one gives it no rest at all, it can become clouded and completely ruined. And through the confusion of <span class="h">da'as</span> [awareness], one can, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, be drawn into sin.</div>

<div class="p">This is why our Sages <span class="h">zichronam livrachah</span> warned about business — that one should be careful not to mention the Name of Heaven in vain, as written in the <span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span>, placing this prohibition adjacent to [the laws of] business. For the prohibition of mentioning the Name of Heaven in vain is also of this same nature. When one mentions the Name of Heaven, one is, as it were, calling upon Him, <span class="h">kivyachol</span> — the Life of all lives — as is explained there [in the <span class="h">Torah</span> of Rabbainu]. Therefore one must not mention the Name of Heaven except in Torah and prayer, for there the vessels and <span class="h">middos</span> [measures] exist to receive the life-force.</div>

<div class="p">And even though the Name itself is the aspect of <span class="h">middos</span>, as is explained there — nonetheless, it is known that there are vessels for vessels and contraction within contraction. What is considered a vessel at a higher level is still, at a lower level, the aspect of simple light without a vessel, and another contraction and vessel must be fashioned to receive it. Therefore it is forbidden to mention the Name of Heaven by itself, except in Torah and prayer, where the other words become vessels to receive the light, as above. But when one mentions the Name of Heaven in vain — without Torah and prayer — it is the aspect of "excessive oil causing the lamp to go out." Therefore mentioning the Name of Heaven in vain is graver than a <span class="h">berachah</span> in vain, as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of "Name of Heaven in vain [<span class="h">l'vatalah</span>]" — the aspect of "destined to be nullified [<span class="h">b'tailah</span>]" as above — for it causes the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, i.e. the ruination of the vessels, as above. Therefore our Sages <span class="h">zichronam livrachah</span> said: <span class="mx">"wherever mention of the Name of Heaven is common, death is common,"</span> for it is the opposite of calling forth life and length of days mentioned above. For that is achieved specifically through the <span class="h">middos</span> and vessels that are made through Torah study, as above. And mentioning the Name of Heaven in vain is the opposite of this, as above. Therefore, at the time of business — which exists to preserve the vessels so they will not be ruined, as above — one must be careful then about mentioning the Name of Heaven in vain, which is a blemish and ruination of the vessels, as above.</div>

<div class="sep">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

<!-- ═══════════════ BRIDGING NOTE: Halachah 2 concluding material ═══════════════ -->
<div class="sn">Brief Summary Appearing at End of Halachah 2 in the Text</div>

<div class="p">[The text presents a brief outline of key topics from <span class="h">Netilas Yadayim</span> and <span class="h">Betzias HaPas</span>, Halachah 2:]</div>

<div class="p">The essential power of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> is in the place where there is holiness. This is [the concept of] <span class="h">chametz</span> and <span class="h">matzah</span>. <span class="h">Netilas yadayim</span> [washing hands] = raising them in the aspect of <span class="v">"let us lift our hearts [with our hands]"</span> — in order to receive for them from Heaven. Therefore one must think thoughts of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> before eating = the aspect of a sigh.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"And you shall not go in haste"</span> — in the final section [of the <span class="h">Torah</span>], because there is the receiving of the Torah; but then [in Mitzrayim] they went in haste, for Pharaoh's authority was still upon them — therefore they ate <span class="h">matzah</span>, as it is written: <span class="v">"they baked the dough,"</span> etc. One must learn [Torah] at the table = the aspect of Torah study. Eating is the aspect of learning, as it is written: <span class="v">"come, buy and eat."</span> As if they ate from the sacrifices of the dead = the opposite of life. The table is the table of kings — <span class="v">"fortunate is your land whose king is a free man, and whose princes eat at the proper time."</span></div>

<div class="p">The washing with water = the aspect of <span class="h">chesed</span> = the aspect of a <span class="h">mikveh</span> of water. A <span class="h">revi'is</span> [quarter-<span class="h">log</span>] = the aspect of <span class="h">Malchus</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">Dalet</span> becoming <span class="h">Hai</span> — <span class="h">da'as</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">revi'is ha'hin</span>. A vessel and the strength of a man [are required] — even when one does not eat with his hands, one must wash; conversely, one who feeds [another] need not [wash]. Ten words in <span class="h">Birchas HaMotzi</span>, and one must also hold [the bread] with all ten fingers — corresponding to <span class="h">Malchus</span> [and] the ministers of tens. The greatest of those reclining is the one who breaks bread. Through eating in a <span class="h">kasher</span> manner, <span class="h">da'as</span> is completed.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Masa Umasan</span>, Halachah 3: its whereabouts is not known to us.</div>

<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary — Halachos 1–2 ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>Halachah 1</strong> (LM I:69): The root of <em>nefashos</em> is in wealth. Jewish money = <em>shem</em> and <em>nefesh</em> = the Name of Hashem. Idolaters' money = the name of other gods = <em>avodah zarah</em>. Partnership mixes these roots. Oaths = the Name of Hashem = clarify money's true ownership. Since idolaters are rooted in <em>sheker</em>, their oaths = name of other gods. Therefore partnership is forbidden.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>Halachah 2</strong> (LM I:35): Business gives rest to the mind after Torah study, preventing "excessive light shattering the vessels." "Torah without work is destined to be nullified and brings sin." Mentioning the Name of Heaven in vain ruins the vessels — worse than a <em>berachah</em> in vain: "excessive oil extinguishes the lamp." "Wherever mention of the Name is common, death is common." During business (which preserves vessels), one must be especially careful about mentioning the Name in vain.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;">The end of Halachah 2 includes a brief outline of key concepts from <em>Netilas Yadayim</em>/<em>Betzias HaPas</em> Halachah 2. <strong>Halachah 3: "its whereabouts is not known to us."</strong></p>
